A `CSS.merge(a, b)` or `a +++ b` whose arguments the ppx can resolve orders
`a`'s competing rules before `b`'s, whatever block defined each atom first.

`sidebar` defines the `@media` column atom before `controls` defines `row`.
`merge(controls, controlsMobile)` still gives column at 600px or narrower.

`narrowGap` defines `column-gap` (autoprefixed with `-webkit-column-gap`)
before `wideGap` defines `gap`. `merge(wideGap, narrowGap)` keeps both
classes, and `column-gap: 0` comes after `gap: 8px`.

`merge(a, b)` gives `b` the result on mobile; the later, unrelated block
writes `@media` first and `padding: 8px` last, and in emotion order its own
declaration comes first too, so the two orders agree.

`base +++ [%css ...]` orders the inline block after `base`.

